    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Why Doubly Linked Lists?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Imagine you're designing a text editor where users can enter and edit text."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now, suppose you want to add the undo and redo options. This requires moving through the memory in both directions."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Unfortunately, singly linked lists only move in a single direction, i.e., forward:"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Singly Linked List Implementation",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "As a result, we can't perform an undo operation using a singly linked list."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Instead, we need to start from scratch and go through each step, which results in O(n) time complexity and is thus not ideal."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "There's a simple and elegant solution: the doubly linked list."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Each node points to the next and previous nodes, forming a two-way chain."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Circular Playlist",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "This speeds up redo and undo operations. We will see how in the upcoming lessons."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Doubly linked lists find applications in various areas like:"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "task scheduling,",
        "cache management",
        "doubly ended queue implementations",
        "and many more"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
